    "text": "A tower <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> stands on a horizontal ground with base <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math> on the ground. The point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> divides the tower in two parts such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. If from a point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> on the ground the angle of elevation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and the part <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi></math> of the tower subtends an angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>, then the height of the tower is&nbsp;",
